```

```{js}
//| viewof: show_dual
const wrap = document.createElement("label");
wrap.className = "tali-input";
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "checkbox";
wrap.append(input, " Show the dual-price split");
return wrap;
```

```{js}
//| label: fig-evening-hour
//| input: soc_a, soc_b, share_a, show_dual
//| fig-cap: "Splits of the fleet's sale, A's share from the left and B's from the right, under what each battery sells alone. The green band holds the splits where neither owner would sell more alone."

// slider values arrive as strings, or undefined before the inputs register
const read = (name, fallback) => {
  const x = Number(tali.value(name));
  return Number.isFinite(x) ? x : fallback;
};
const pa = read("soc_a", 90);
const pb = read("soc_b", 20);
const share = read("share_a", 50) / 100;
const showDual = tali.value("show_dual") === true;

// whole percents, so a tie between charge and space is an exact comparison
const fleetChargePct = pa + pb;
const fleetSpacePct = 200 - pa - pb;
const aloneA = Math.min(pa, 100 - pa) / 100;
const aloneB = Math.min(pb, 100 - pb) / 100;
const total = Math.min(fleetChargePct, fleetSpacePct) / 100;

// pro rata: the sale in proportion to each battery's charge
const proA = fleetChargePct === 0 ? 0 : (total * pa) / fleetChargePct;

// dual prices: pay for the side that runs out; at a tie any mix of the two is optimal
let dualLo, dualHi;
if (fleetSpacePct < fleetChargePct) {
  dualLo = dualHi = (100 - pa) / 100;
} else if (fleetChargePct < fleetSpacePct) {
  dualLo = dualHi = pa / 100;
} else {
  dualLo = Math.min(pa, 100 - pa) / 100;
  dualHi = Math.max(pa, 100 - pa) / 100;
}
const yourA = share * total;
const coreLo = aloneA;
const coreHi = total - aloneB;

// the splits against the core band; the top row leaves the extra between the two own sales empty
const rows = [
  ["Your split", yourA, yourA],
  ["Pro rata", proA, proA],
];
if (showDual) rows.push(["Dual prices", dualLo, dualHi]);
const segments = [
  { row: "Sells alone", owner: "A", x1: 0, x2: aloneA },
  { row: "Sells alone", owner: "B", x1: total - aloneB, x2: total },
  ...rows.flatMap(([row, lo, hi]) => [
    { row, owner: "A", x1: 0, x2: lo },
    { row, owner: "A or B", x1: lo, x2: hi },
    { row, owner: "B", x1: hi, x2: total },
  ]),
].filter((d) => d.x2 - d.x1 > 1e-9);
rows.unshift(["Sells alone"]);
const bottom = Plot.plot({
  width: 640,
  height: 70 + 33 * rows.length,
  marginLeft: 90,
  x: { domain: [0, Math.max(total, 0.05)], label: "MW credited →" },
  y: { domain: rows.map((r) => r[0]), label: null },
  color: { domain: ["A", "B", "A or B"], range: ["steelblue", "darkorange", "#9ca3af"], legend: true },
  marks: [
    Plot.rect([{ x1: coreLo, x2: coreHi }], { x1: "x1", x2: "x2", fill: "mediumseagreen", fillOpacity: 0.3 }),
    Plot.barX(segments, { x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y: "row", fill: "owner", insetTop: 7, insetBottom: 7 }),
    Plot.ruleX([coreLo, coreHi], { stroke: "mediumseagreen", strokeWidth: 2, strokeDasharray: "4,3" }),
  ],
});

// readout in whole hundredths of a MW: B gets the rest of the rounded sale, so the printed shares add up
const cents = (x) => Math.round(x * 100);
const mw = (c) => (c / 100).toFixed(2);
const totalC = cents(total);
const verdict = (aC) =>
  aC < cents(aloneA) ? "A's owner would leave" : totalC - aC < cents(aloneB) ? "B's owner would leave" : "both stay";
const splitText = (a) => `A ${mw(cents(a))} MW, B ${mw(totalC - cents(a))} MW, ${verdict(cents(a))}`;
let line = `A at ${pa} %, B at ${pb} %. `;
if (totalC === 0) {
  line += "The fleet sells nothing in this hour, so there is nothing to split.";
} else {
  line += `Your split: ${splitText(yourA)}. Pro rata: ${splitText(proA)}.`;
  if (showDual) {
    line += dualLo === dualHi
      ? ` Dual prices: ${splitText(dualLo)}.`
      : ` Dual prices: A anywhere from ${mw(cents(dualLo))} to ${mw(cents(dualHi))} MW.`;
  }
}
const text = document.createElement("p");
text.style.cssText = "font-size:0.9em;margin:0.4em 0 0;";
text.textContent = line;

const out = document.createElement("div");
out.append(bottom, text);
return out;
```

With the starting charges, pro rata gives A 0.74 MW and B 0.16 MW. B sells 0.2 MW on its own, so the factory's owner would take B out of the fleet. For A the band runs from the 0.1 MW it sells alone up to 0.7 MW, where B is left with its own 0.2 MW. A split by own sale, in proportion to what each battery sells alone, gives A 0.3 MW and B 0.6 MW, inside the band. With two batteries that rule always stays inside, because the pair never sells less than the two apart.

# Dual prices

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Notation used in this post

| Symbol | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| $c_i$ | Charge of battery $i$ in MWh, also its upward capacity in MW |
| $1 - c_i$ | Empty space of battery $i$ in MWh, its downward capacity in MW |
| $S$ | A group of batteries |
| $v(S)$ | MW the group $S$ sells on its own |
| $x_i$ | MW of the fleet's sale credited to battery $i$ |
| $b$ | The fleet's bid in MW |
| $u_i, d_i$ | Upward and downward capacity battery $i$ offers, in MW |
| $\lambda, \mu$ | Dual prices of one MWh of charge and one MWh of space, in MW of the sale |
| $\alpha, \beta$ | Prices on the two limits that cover the bid, in the full dual |
:::

## The core

Write $v(S)$ for the MW that a group $S$ of batteries sells on its own, such as the 0.9 MW of the evening hour's pair. With $c_i$ the charge of battery $i$ in MWh,

$$v(S) = \min\Big(\sum_{i \in S} c_i,\; \sum_{i \in S} (1 - c_i)\Big),$$

and the solid lines in @fig-pooling mark its values in the evening hour:

$$v(\{A\}) = 0.1, \qquad v(\{B\}) = 0.2, \qquad v(\{A, B\}) = 0.9.$$

Game theory calls a setting like this a cooperative game. The owners are the players, and $v(S)$ is what each group of them can get on its own. A split credits battery $i$ with $x_i$ MW of the fleet's sale, and the green band in the demo holds the splits with

$$x_A \ge 0.1, \qquad x_B \ge 0.2, \qquad x_A + x_B = 0.9.$$

These splits form the core of the game. With more batteries, a group of owners can also leave together and sell on its own. A split is in the core when every group gets at least what it sells on its own and the shares add up to the fleet's sale:

$$\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i \in S} x_i &\ge v(S) \quad \text{for every group } S, \\
\sum_i x_i &= \text{the fleet's sale}.
\end{aligned}$$

## The fleet as a linear program

In the evening hour the fleet chooses a bid $b$ and how much upward capacity $u_i$ and downward capacity $d_i$ each battery offers, up to its charge bar and its space bar in @fig-pooling. A can offer at most 0.9 MW upwards and 0.1 MW downwards, B at most 0.2 MW and 0.8 MW, and the bid has to be covered in both directions. For any charges this is a linear program, a linear objective maximised under linear limits:

$$\begin{aligned}
\max_{b,\, u,\, d \,\ge\, 0} \quad & b \\
\text{subject to} \quad & b \le \sum_i u_i, \quad b \le \sum_i d_i \\
& u_i \le c_i, \quad d_i \le 1 - c_i
\end{aligned}$$

The best bid is 0.9 MW. It uses all of the fleet's space and leaves 0.2 MWh of charge spare.

## Prices for charge and space

Every linear program comes with a second one, called its dual, which puts a price on each limit. The dual reads as the problem of an imagined buyer who wants to take all of the fleet's charge and space instead of letting it sell reserve. The buyer looks for the cheapest prices the fleet would accept.

The fleet's program has a limit on each battery's charge and on each battery's space, and two more limits that make the bid covered in both directions. A MWh of charge covers an upward MW in whichever battery it sits, so the cheapest prices put one price on every battery's charge. For the same reason they put one price on every battery's space. The prices on the two bid limits come out equal to these two. That leaves $\lambda$ for a MWh of charge and $\mu$ for a MWh of space, each counted in MW of the sale.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## The full dual, with one price per limit

Give the limit $b \le \sum_i u_i$ the price $\alpha$, the limit $b \le \sum_i d_i$ the price $\beta$, and battery $i$'s limits $u_i \le c_i$ and $d_i \le 1 - c_i$ the prices $\lambda_i$ and $\mu_i$. The rules for writing the dual of a maximisation with $\le$ limits and nonnegative variables [@bertsimas1997] give

$$\begin{aligned}
\min_{\alpha,\, \beta,\, \lambda_i,\, \mu_i \ge 0} \quad & \sum_i \big(\lambda_i c_i + \mu_i (1 - c_i)\big) \\
\text{subject to} \quad & \alpha + \beta \ge 1, \quad \lambda_i \ge \alpha, \quad \mu_i \ge \beta \quad \text{for every } i.
\end{aligned}$$

The objective never falls when a $\lambda_i$ or $\mu_i$ rises, so a cheapest solution sets $\lambda_i = \alpha$ and $\mu_i = \beta$ in every battery. With $\lambda = \alpha$ and $\mu = \beta$ this is the two-price dual below.
:::

One MW of reserve uses one MWh of charge and one MWh of space for the hour. The buyer has to pay at least 1 MW for that pair, $\lambda + \mu \ge 1$, or the fleet would rather sell reserve. The dual looks for the cheapest prices that meet this condition:

$$\begin{aligned}
\min_{\lambda,\, \mu \ge 0} \quad & \lambda \sum_i c_i + \mu \sum_i (1 - c_i) \\
\text{subject to} \quad & \lambda + \mu \ge 1
\end{aligned}$$

Prices that add up to more than 1 only raise the payment, so the cheapest ones add up to exactly 1. In the evening hour the fleet holds 1.1 MWh of charge and 0.9 MWh of space, and with $\mu = 1 - \lambda$ the buyer pays

$$1.1\,\lambda + 0.9\,(1 - \lambda) = 0.9 + 0.2\,\lambda.$$

The payment is lowest at $\lambda = 0$, where charge is free and a MWh of space is worth 1 MW. The fleet is then paid 0.9 MW, exactly what it sells. @fig-dual-prices draws the fleet's payment for every price of charge, and what the same prices pay A and B.

```{js}
//| viewof: dual_soc_b
const wrap = document.createElement("label");
wrap.className = "tali-input";
wrap.append("Battery B's charge % ");
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "range"; input.min = "0"; input.max = "40"; input.step = "1"; input.value = "20";
wrap.append(input);
return wrap;
```

```{js}
//| label: fig-dual-prices
//| input: dual_soc_b
//| fig-cap: "What the batteries are paid when a MWh of charge costs λ and a MWh of space costs 1 − λ, with A at 90 %. Each solid line is what a battery or the fleet is paid, and the dashed line of the same colour is what it sells. With B at 20 % this is the evening hour. Move B's charge."

// slider value arrives as a string, or undefined before the input registers
const read = (name, fallback) => {
  const x = Number(tali.value(name));
  return Number.isFinite(x) ? x : fallback;
};
// whole percents, so a tie between the fleet's charge and space is an exact comparison
const pa = 90;
const pb = read("dual_soc_b", 20);
const groups = [
  { who: "Fleet", c: pa + pb, s: 200 - pa - pb, color: "currentColor" },
  { who: "A", c: pa, s: 100 - pa, color: "steelblue" },
  { who: "B", c: pb, s: 100 - pb, color: "darkorange" },
].map((g) => ({ ...g, charge: g.c / 100, space: g.s / 100, sells: Math.min(g.c, g.s) / 100 }));
const fleet = groups[0];
const prices = d3.range(0, 1.0001, 0.01);
const paid = groups.flatMap((g) =>
  prices.map((lambda) => ({ ...g, lambda, paid: g.charge * lambda + g.space * (1 - lambda) }))
);
// dashed-line labels sit where no payment line crosses them, offset in MW
const floorLabels = [
  { ...groups[0], at: 0.5, offset: -0.05, label: `Fleet sells ${fleet.sells.toFixed(2)}` },
  { ...groups[1], at: 0.7, offset: 0.04, label: `A sells ${groups[1].sells.toFixed(2)} alone` },
  { ...groups[2], at: 0.45, offset: 0.04, label: `B sells ${groups[2].sells.toFixed(2)} alone` },
];
// the cheapest price of charge: 0 when the fleet is short of space, 1 when short of charge, any at a tie
const tie = fleet.c === fleet.s;
const cheapest = tie ? [] : [{ lambda: fleet.s < fleet.c ? 0 : 1, paid: fleet.sells }];
// end labels: lift the fleet's when it would sit on A's, offset in MW
const close = Math.abs(fleet.charge - groups[1].charge) < 0.06;
const endOffset = { Fleet: close ? 0.04 : 0, A: close ? -0.03 : 0, B: 0 };

return Plot.plot({
  width: 640,
  height: 300,
  marginRight: 50,
  x: { domain: [0, 1], label: "price of a MWh of charge, λ (space costs 1 − λ) →" },
  y: { domain: [0, 1.35], label: "MW paid", grid: true },
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY(groups, { y: "sells", stroke: "color", strokeWidth: 1.5, strokeDasharray: "4,3" }),
    Plot.text(floorLabels, {
      x: "at",
      y: (d) => d.sells + d.offset,
      text: "label",
      fill: "color",
      fontSize: 11,
    }),
    Plot.line(paid, { x: "lambda", y: "paid", z: "who", stroke: "color", strokeWidth: 2 }),
    Plot.text(groups, {
      x: 1,
      y: (d) => d.charge + endOffset[d.who],
      text: "who",
      dx: 8,
      textAnchor: "start",
      fill: "color",
    }),
    Plot.dot(cheapest, { x: "lambda", y: "paid", r: 4, fill: "currentColor" }),
    Plot.text(tie ? [{ x: 0.5, y: fleet.sells + 0.06 }] : [], {
      x: "x",
      y: "y",
      text: () => "flat: every λ gives the cheapest payment",
      fill: "currentColor",
      fontSize: 11,
    }),
  ],
});
```

No prices with $\lambda + \mu \ge 1$ pay the fleet less than it sells. The bid is at most the fleet's charge and at most its space, so for any such prices

$$\lambda \sum_i c_i + \mu \sum_i (1 - c_i) \;\ge\; (\lambda + \mu)\, b \;\ge\; b.$$

In every linear program, prices that meet the dual's limits pay at least what any allowed solution earns. This is called weak duality [@bertsimas1997]. Strong duality says that the cheapest prices pay exactly the best solution's value, and it holds for every linear program that has an optimal solution [@bertsimas1997]. For the fleet it can be checked directly. The payment $\lambda \sum_i c_i + (1 - \lambda) \sum_i (1 - c_i)$ is a straight line in $\lambda$, so its lowest value sits at $\lambda = 0$ or $\lambda = 1$ and equals the smaller of the fleet's charge and space. That is what the fleet sells.

The cheapest prices also say what a little more of each limit would be worth, per MWh. If the fleet had 0.1 MWh more space, it could sell 0.1 MW more. A MWh of space is therefore worth $\mu = 1$ MW, until the space reaches the 1.1 MWh of charge. The fleet has 0.2 MWh of charge to spare, so more charge would add nothing, and $\lambda = 0$. Prices with this meaning are called shadow prices.

## The dual split is in the core

The dual split pays each battery for its own charge and space at the cheapest prices, which in the evening hour are $\lambda = 0$ and $\mu = 1$:

$$x_i = \lambda c_i + \mu (1 - c_i).$$

Each battery is then paid for its space, so A gets 0.1 MW and B gets 0.8 MW. The payments add up to the 0.9 MW. A gets the 0.1 MW it sells alone and B more than its 0.2 MW, so the split is in the core, on the left edge of the band. Tick the box under the sliders of @fig-evening-hour to see it, then move the charges.

With $\lambda + \mu = 1$, the payment to a battery, or to any group of batteries, is a weighted average of its charge and its space. An average is never below the smaller of the two numbers, and the smaller one is what the group sells on its own. In @fig-dual-prices the A and B lines never drop below their dashed lines, and at the cheapest prices strong duality makes the payments add up to the fleet's sale. Every price pair with $\lambda + \mu = 1$ gives each group at least what it sells. Only the cheapest also pays out no more than the fleet earns. At $\lambda = 0.5$ the payments would add up to 1.0 MW against a sale of 0.9 MW.

Owen's theorem extends this to every linear production game [@owen1975]. In such a game each owner brings resources, here charge and space, and a group sells the most that its pooled resources allow under linear limits. Paying each owner for its resources at any optimal solution of the whole fleet's dual gives a split in the core.

The dual prices take the factory owner's side. A's 0.9 MWh of charge could cover the whole bid alone, and so could 0.7 MWh from A with B's 0.2 MWh, so no battery's charge limited the sale. Charge gets a price of zero, and A is paid only for its 0.1 MWh of space. Every split in the band keeps both owners, so this is the rule's choice among them.

The cell below checks the theorem on three batteries: A and B as in the evening hour, and a third battery C at 60 %. It solves the linear program for every group with CVXPY, a Python optimisation library, and reads the fleet's dual prices from the solver.

```{python}
#| code-fold: true
#| code-summary: "CVXPY: solve every group, then compare three splits"
import itertools

import cvxpy as cp
import numpy as np

charge = np.array([0.90, 0.20, 0.60])  # MWh stored in three 1 MWh batteries
names = ["A", "B", "C"]
n = len(charge)


def fleet_sale(group):
    """MW of FCR-N the batteries in `group` sell together, and their dual prices."""
    inside = np.isin(np.arange(n), list(group)).astype(float)
    up = cp.Variable(n, nonneg=True)
    down = cp.Variable(n, nonneg=True)
    bid = cp.Variable(nonneg=True)
    has_charge = up <= inside * charge
    has_space = down <= inside * (1 - charge)
    problem = cp.Problem(
        cp.Maximize(bid),
        [bid <= cp.sum(up), bid <= cp.sum(down), has_charge, has_space],
    )
    problem.solve()
    return problem.value, has_charge.dual_value, has_space.dual_value


groups = [frozenset(g) for r in range(1, n + 1) for g in itertools.combinations(range(n), r)]
v = {g: fleet_sale(g)[0] for g in groups}

# pro rata: the fleet's sale in proportion to each battery's charge
total, price_charge, price_space = fleet_sale(range(n))
pro_rata = total * charge / charge.sum()

# by own sale: the fleet's sale in proportion to what each battery sells alone
alone = np.array([v[frozenset([i])] for i in range(n)])
by_own_sale = total * alone / alone.sum()

# dual split: each battery's charge and space at the fleet's dual prices
dual = price_charge * charge + price_space * (1 - charge)

# one row per group and split for the figure below (define() is Taliesin's bridge to it)
splits = (("pro rata", pro_rata), ("by own sale", by_own_sale), ("dual split", dual))
rows = [
    {"group": "+".join(names[i] for i in sorted(g)), "split": split,
     "paid": float(pay[sorted(g)].sum()), "alone": float(v[g])}
    for g in groups
    if len(g) < n  # every split pays the whole fleet its full sale
    for split, pay in splits
]
define(splits=rows)
```

```{js}
//| label: fig-splits
//| fig-cap: "What each group of batteries is paid under three splits, with A and B as in the evening hour and C at 60 %. The tick in each row marks what the group sells on its own. A red bar stops short of its tick, and that group would leave the fleet. Every split pays the whole fleet A+B+C its full sale, so its row is left out."

// one row per group and split, from the CVXPY cell above
const rows = tali.value("splits");
if (!Array.isArray(rows)) return document.createElement("div");
const stays = (d) => d.paid >= d.alone - 1e-6;

return Plot.plot({
  width: 640,
  height: 200,
  marginLeft: 40,
  fx: {
    domain: ["pro rata", "by own sale", "dual split"],
    label: null,
    tickFormat: (d) => d[0].toUpperCase() + d.slice(1),
  },
  y: { domain: ["A", "B", "C", "A+B", "A+C", "B+C"], label: null },
  x: { domain: [0, 1.25], ticks: [0, 0.5, 1], label: "MW", grid: true },
  marks: [
    Plot.barX(rows.filter(stays), { fx: "split", y: "group", x: "paid", fill: "mediumseagreen", fillOpacity: 0.5 }),
    Plot.barX(rows.filter((d) => !stays(d)), { fx: "split", y: "group", x: "paid", fill: "firebrick" }),
    Plot.tickX(rows, { fx: "split", y: "group", x: "alone", stroke: "currentColor", strokeWidth: 2 }),
  ],
});
```

In @fig-splits, B and the pairs A+B and B+C would leave under pro rata. The split by own sale keeps every single battery, but it gives the pair A+B less than the 0.9 MW the pair sells. The dual split gives every group at least what it sells. It needs only one solve of the whole fleet's program, and the cell solves the other groups only to check it.

# Failure modes

**Ties.** Move B's charge in @fig-dual-prices to 10 %. The fleet then holds 1.0 MWh of charge and 1.0 MWh of space, its line is flat, and every price of charge gives the cheapest payment. The dual split depends on which prices the solver returns, and A can get anything from 0.1 to 0.9 MW. With the box ticked, A at 90 % and B at 10 %, the dual row of @fig-evening-hour turns grey for the part of the sale that could go to either owner. Near the tie the split jumps. With B at 9 % the fleet is short of charge and A is paid 0.9 MW. Two points higher, at 11 %, it is short of space and A is paid 0.1 MW.

**The edge of the core.** The dual split pays only for the limit the fleet is short of. When space is short, every battery is paid for its space. A battery that is more than half full sells exactly its space on its own, so it gets none of the extra. In the evening hour A's owner gains nothing from the fleet, and a rival fleet paying slightly more would win A.

# Summary

Two batteries sell more together than apart because one battery's charge pairs with the other's space. Pro rata can pay an owner, or a group of owners, less than they would sell alone. The fleet's sale is a linear program. Its dual puts a price on each MWh of charge and space, equal to what a little more of it would add. Paying every battery for its own charge and space at those prices gives a split in the core (Owen). The split jumps where the fleet's charge equals its space, and when space is short, a battery more than half full gets none of the extra.

What to read next:

- Owen [@owen1975], for the theorem and its proof.
- Bertsimas and Tsitsiklis [@bertsimas1997], chapter 4, cover duality and shadow prices.
- Shapley [@shapley1953] defines the best-known other split. It averages what each battery adds over every order in which the batteries could join.
